---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, href } from '../../data/site';
import CompareMatrix from '../../components/charts/CompareMatrix.astro';
import CostBars from '../../components/charts/CostBars.astro';
import DecompositionFigure from '../../components/charts/DecompositionFigure.astro';

const landscape = [
  {
    tool: 'Intel VTune Threading Analysis',
    overlap: 'The closest overlap. It already separates preemption wait from inactive sync wait on Linux.',
    difference: 'Intel-branded and a tool rather than a component. It cannot be linked into somebody else\u2019s engine, and its output is VTune\u2019s model rather than yours.',
  },
  {
    tool: 'Perfetto',
    overlap: 'A genuinely embeddable C and C++ SDK, with low overhead and OS scheduling states. The most credible competitor.',
    difference: 'It is a trace. Buffers, IPC and a background writer are the design, and the consumer parses a trace afterwards rather than reading a counter in-process.',
  },
  {
    tool: 'Arm Streamline',
    overlap: 'System-wide profiling including scheduling analysis, on Linux and embedded targets.',
    difference: 'A tool, and architecture-aligned. Not a portable component you vendor into your own product.',
  },
  {
    tool: 'easy_profiler, nTrace and similar',
    overlap: 'Low-overhead embeddable libraries that already emit thread context-switch events.',
    difference: 'They report the events. Deciding what the resulting time means, and proving the arithmetic holds, is the part that is missing.',
  },
];

const searches = [
  ['Tool survey', 'done', 'The five tools above, read from their own documentation rather than from their marketing.'],
  ['Literature', 'done', 'The measurement method follows the published benchmarking rules; the attribution itself relies on documented OS primitives.'],
  ['Patent search', 'not done', 'The one patent the source brief cited is behind an account and could not be located. A full freedom-to-operate search is a precondition for any commercial licensing, and it has not been run.'],
];
---

<Base
  title="Prior art and the competitive posture — PACE"
  description="Who already does this, where they are stronger, and the claim PACE actually makes after the premise it started from was found to be false."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="breadcrumb"><a href={href()}>PACE</a> / <a href={href('research/')}>research</a> / prior art</div>
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="section-label">Research · prior art</div>
      <h1>The premise this started from was false, and it is still on the record</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        PACE began with the belief that no existing profiler separates time-on-CPU from
        time-lost-to-preemption. Intel VTune already does, and it has for a while. The correct
        response to that is not to reword the marketing — it is to publish the correction and state
        the narrower claim that survives, which is what this page does.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">The comparison</div>
      <h2>Where they overlap, and where they do not</h2>
      <CompareMatrix />
      <p>
        Read the matrix as five separate claims rather than as a score. The two columns where the
        incumbents lose are the two that matter: a component you link into your own product, and no
        requirement for elevated privilege. Everything else is a distinction without a difference.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">The landscape</div>
      <h2>Each competitor, and the honest reason it is still a competitor</h2>
      <div class="flow">
        {
          landscape.map((item) => (
            <div class="flow-row">
              <div class="flow-icon"><i class="ph ph-crosshair" style="font-size:20px"></i></div>
              <div>
                <h3>{item.tool}</h3>
                <p>{item.overlap}</p>
                <p><strong>Where PACE differs:</strong> {item.difference}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">The risk nobody should pretend away</div>
        <p>
          Every one of these vendors could add this capability themselves, and several have the
          scheduler plumbing to do it faster than this project can. That is a real risk and it is
          written down as one in the plan. What it does not do is make the measurement wrong — and a
          measurement that other people can re-run is the only defence against it that holds.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Cost, which is where the argument is winnable</div>
      <h2>What the difference costs per sample</h2>
      <CostBars />
      <p>
        The comparison at the top of the matrix is about capability; this one is about whether a
        vendor can afford to leave it switched on. A trace-based SDK pays for a buffer and a writer
        whether or not anybody reads the trace. A counter that returns in the hundreds of nanoseconds
        can run on every sample of a production frame.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Prior art</div>
      <h2>What was searched, and what was not</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Search</th>
              <th>State</th>
              <th>What it found or why it stopped</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              searches.map(([what, state, note]) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{what}</td>
                  <td class={state === 'done' ? 'ok' : 'warn'}>{state}</td>
                  <td class="dim" style="white-space:normal">{note}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">The patent posture, stated plainly</div>
        <p>
          This design relies on operating-system primitives that are documented and public, which is
          a materially different position from a patented mechanism. That is an argument, not a
          clearance. No patent number is printed anywhere in this project, because the one the source
          brief cited could not be retrieved and a number that cannot be checked is worse than no
          number.
        </p>
      </div>
      <DecompositionFigure />
      <p>
        What is being claimed as the contribution is the implementation and the evidence, not the
        idea. The decomposition is textbook; making it hold exactly, per sample, from a library you
        can link into somebody else's engine without privilege, is the part that took the work.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Next</div>
      <h2>The claim in one paragraph</h2>
      <p>
        PACE is a counter. It measures a thread's wall time as the sum of the time it ran, the time
        the scheduler took the core away, and the time it was not runnable. It is portable,
        embeddable, privilege-free and in-process, and the arithmetic is verified on every sample
        against sources that do not come from PACE. It is not the first tool to know the difference
        between a preemption and a wait, and it does not claim to be.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn primary" href={href('research/accuracy/')}>
          <i class="ph ph-ruler"></i>
          The accuracy method
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href={href('service/')}>
          <i class="ph ph-activity"></i>
          Test it on a trace
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
